# Cache and security headers for the deployed site. Copied into dist/ by Vite, like
# _redirects beside it.
#
# WHY THE CACHING MATTERS HERE SPECIFICALLY. Netlify's default is
# `max-age=0, must-revalidate`, so every asset costs a revalidation round trip on every
# load even when it has not changed. WI-22 exists because this app is used on rural cell
# data; 40 chunks each paying a round trip is the same problem the code splitting was
# meant to solve, arriving from the other direction.
#
# Vite fingerprints every file in /assets/, so a changed file is a changed URL. That is
# what makes `immutable` safe rather than reckless: nothing under /assets/ is ever
# rewritten in place.

/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# index.html is the opposite case and must NOT be cached. It is the only file that names
# which hashed chunks exist, so a stale copy points at chunk filenames a deploy has
# already deleted. That is exactly R-6's chunk-load error — the one case where "reload the
# page" is the correct advice — and there is no reason to manufacture it.

/index.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# Two headers that cost nothing and close ordinary footguns.
#
# NOT SET, deliberately: Content-Security-Policy. The PDF and print reports build an HTML
# string and open it as a same-origin blob URL (guardrail 2), and Tailwind ships inline
# styles. A CSP strict enough to be worth having would break report printing, and the
# control that actually protects that path is `esc()` on every interpolation — which is
# SEC-2, and is already in place. A CSP here would be reassurance rather than defence.

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
